window.BOOK_INDEX = [{"p":"index.html","c":0,"t":"AI Tools and Applications","h":"","a":"","x":"This is a small book for the course AI Tools and Applications. It is for first year B.Tech students. It is common to all branches. The book is written in simple English. The sentences are short. New words are explained at the end of each chapter."},{"p":"index.html","c":0,"t":"AI Tools and Applications","h":"How to read this book","a":"how-to-read-this-book","x":"Read one chapter at a time. Each chapter takes about 10 minutes. Every chapter has a Try it box with links. Open the links and try the steps yourself. This is the best way to learn AI tools. Every chapter ends with a New words box. Read it if a word is new to you. The last chapter is a guide to the 20 lab experiments. Use it in the lab."},{"p":"index.html","c":0,"t":"AI Tools and Applications","h":"What you will be able to do","a":"what-you-will-be-able-to-do","x":"After this course, you will be able to: Explain what Generative AI tools are and where they are used. (CO1). Write good prompts to get useful answers from AI. (CO2). Use AI tools for writing code, fixing code and making documents. (CO3). Make images, greeting cards and presentations with AI. (CO4). Design a small AI application in a safe, legal and ethical way. (CO5)."},{"p":"index.html","c":0,"t":"AI Tools and Applications","h":"The five units","a":"the-five-units","x":"Unit Topic Chapters. I Introduction to AI tools 1 to 6. II Prompt engineering, writing, documents, greeting cards 7 to 11. III AI tools for coding 12 to 15. IV Presentations with AI 16 to 18. V AI applications, automation and future trends 19 to 21."},{"p":"index.html","c":0,"t":"AI Tools and Applications","h":"What you need","a":"what-you-need","x":"A laptop or a phone with internet. A Google account (Gmail). This gives you Gemini, NotebookLM and Google AI Studio. A free ChatGPT account. Go to chatgpt.com and sign up. A free Canva account and a free Gamma account. You will make these in Units II and IV. All the tools in this book have a free plan. You do not need to pay for anything in this course."},{"p":"index.html","c":0,"t":"AI Tools and Applications","h":"Books from the syllabus","a":"books-from-the-syllabus","x":"Generative AI 360 by Hitesh Motwani, ZebraLearn, 2025. ChatGPT and other Generative AI Tools by Hem Raj, Shashwat Publication, 2025. Foundations of Artificial Intelligence and Prompt Engineering by Manish Rana and others, Swastik International Publication, 2025. Hands-On Large Language Models by Jay Alammar and Maarten Grootendorst, O'Reilly, 2024."},{"p":"01-what-is-ai.html","c":1,"t":"What is AI?","h":"A simple definition","a":"a-simple-definition","x":"AI means Artificial Intelligence. Artificial means made by humans. Intelligence means the ability to learn, understand and solve problems. So AI is a computer program that can do things which normally need human intelligence. For example: Understanding your voice when you say \"Ok Google\". Finding your friend's face in a photo. Suggesting the next video on YouTube. Finding the fastest route from Gajuwaka to RK Beach on Google Maps. Writing an email when you give it a few words. A normal program follows fixed rules written by a programmer. An AI program learns from examples. This is the big difference."},{"p":"01-what-is-ai.html","c":1,"t":"What is AI?","h":"A short background","a":"a-short-background","x":"AI is not new. Here is a very short history. 1950: Alan Turing asked, \"Can machines think?\" He suggested a test. If a machine can chat like a human, we can call it intelligent. 1956: The name \"Artificial Intelligence\" was used for the first time at a meeting in Dartmouth, USA. 1960s to 1980s: Early AI used rules written by experts. It worked for small problems. It failed for big ones. People lost interest. This time is called the \"AI winter\". 1997: IBM's Deep Blue computer beat the world chess champion, Garry Kasparov. 2012: A new method called deep learning became very good at seeing images. AI started to grow fast. 2017: Google researchers made the Transformer. This is the design used by ChatGPT and Gemini today. 2022: ChatGPT was released. In two months, 100 million people used it. This started the Generative AI wave. Today: AI can write, draw, talk, code and even make videos."},{"p":"01-what-is-ai.html","c":1,"t":"What is AI?","h":"Three kinds of AI","a":"three-kinds-of-ai","x":"Kind Meaning Example. Narrow AI Does one job well Google Maps, spam filter, face unlock. Generative AI Creates new text, images, audio or code ChatGPT, Gemini. General AI (AGI) Can do any task a human can do Does not exist yet. All the AI we use today is narrow AI or generative AI. AGI is still a dream."},{"p":"01-what-is-ai.html","c":1,"t":"What is AI?","h":"Why should an engineer learn AI tools?","a":"why-should-an-engineer-learn-ai-tools","x":"You will use AI tools in every branch. A civil engineer can ask AI to check a report. A mechanical engineer can ask AI to write a Python script to read sensor data. A chemical engineer can ask AI to explain a reaction. AI tools are like a calculator. They do not replace you. They make you faster. Think of AI as a smart helper. It is fast and knows many things. But it can make mistakes. You are the engineer. You must check its work."},{"p":"01-what-is-ai.html","c":1,"t":"What is AI?","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Open chatgpt.com or gemini.google.com. Type: \"Explain AI to a first year engineering student in Visakhapatnam in 5 simple sentences.\". Ask the same tool: \"Give 5 examples of AI that I use every day on my phone.\". Watch this short video: What is AI? – CrashCourse."},{"p":"01-what-is-ai.html","c":1,"t":"What is AI?","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Artificial — made by humans, not natural. Intelligence — the ability to learn, understand and solve problems. Program — a set of instructions that a computer follows. Rule — a fixed instruction like \"if X then do Y\". Deep learning — a way for computers to learn from a lot of examples, using many layers of maths. Transformer — the design (architecture) used inside ChatGPT and Gemini. Narrow AI — AI that does only one kind of job. AGI — Artificial General Intelligence; an AI that can do anything a human can. Not real yet."},{"p":"02-how-ai-learns.html","c":2,"t":"How AI learns","h":"Data, model, prediction","a":"data-model-prediction","x":"Most AI today works in three steps. Data. We collect many examples. For example, 10,000 photos of cats and 10,000 photos of dogs. Training. The computer looks at the examples again and again. It slowly adjusts numbers inside itself until it gets most answers right. The result is called a model. Prediction. We give the model a new photo. It says \"cat\" or \"dog\". This is called a prediction, or an output. The whole method is called Machine Learning (ML). The machine learns from data. Nobody writes the rules by hand."},{"p":"02-how-ai-learns.html","c":2,"t":"How AI learns","h":"A small example","a":"a-small-example","x":"Suppose you want to predict the price of a flat in Madhurawada. You collect data: size of the flat, floor, distance from the beach and the price. You give this data to an ML model. It learns the pattern. Then you give it a new flat. It predicts the price. The model is not magic. It only finds patterns in the data you gave it. If your data is bad, the model is bad. There is a saying: garbage in, garbage out."},{"p":"02-how-ai-learns.html","c":2,"t":"How AI learns","h":"Three ways of learning","a":"three-ways-of-learning","x":"Way How it works Example. Supervised learning Data has the correct answers (labels) Photos labelled \"cat\" or \"dog\". Unsupervised learning Data has no labels; the model finds groups Grouping customers by what they buy. Reinforcement learning The model gets a reward when it does well A game-playing AI, a robot learning to walk."},{"p":"02-how-ai-learns.html","c":2,"t":"How AI learns","h":"Neural networks","a":"neural-networks","x":"A neural network is a type of model. It is loosely inspired by the brain. It has many small units called neurons, arranged in layers. Each neuron does a small calculation. Together, millions of them can learn very complex patterns. When a network has many layers, we call it deep learning. Deep learning is behind almost all modern AI: voice assistants, face unlock, self-driving cars and ChatGPT."},{"p":"02-how-ai-learns.html","c":2,"t":"How AI learns","h":"Training needs a lot of power","a":"training-needs-a-lot-of-power","x":"Training a big model needs: A lot of data (the whole internet, in some cases). A lot of computers with special chips called GPUs. A lot of electricity and money. This is why only big companies like Google, OpenAI, Meta and Anthropic train the largest models. But anyone can use them. That is what this course is about."},{"p":"02-how-ai-learns.html","c":2,"t":"How AI learns","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Play Quick, Draw! by Google. You draw. The AI guesses. Every drawing becomes training data. Try Teachable Machine. Train your own image model in 5 minutes using your webcam. No code needed. Ask Gemini: \"Explain supervised learning with an example from a Vizag fish market.\"."},{"p":"02-how-ai-learns.html","c":2,"t":"How AI learns","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Data — facts, numbers, text or images that a computer can store. Model — the result of training; the \"brain\" that gives predictions. Training — the process of learning from data. Prediction — the answer a model gives for a new input. Label — the correct answer attached to a piece of data. Pattern — something that repeats in data. Neural network — a model made of many small connected units (neurons). Layer — a group of neurons in a network. GPU — Graphics Processing Unit; a chip that is very fast at the maths AI needs."},{"p":"03-generative-ai-and-llms.html","c":3,"t":"Generative AI and LLMs","h":"What is Generative AI?","a":"what-is-generative-ai","x":"Old AI mainly classified things. Is this a cat or a dog? Is this email spam or not? Generative AI is different. It creates new things. It can write a poem, draw a picture, compose music, make a video or write a program. The word generate means to make. Input you give Generative AI makes. \"Write a poem about Rushikonda beach\" A new poem. \"A fishing boat at sunrise, oil painting\" A new image. \"Write a C program to add two numbers\" New code. A 20-page PDF A short summary."},{"p":"03-generative-ai-and-llms.html","c":3,"t":"Generative AI and LLMs","h":"What is an LLM?","a":"what-is-an-llm","x":"An LLM is a Large Language Model. It is the engine inside ChatGPT, Gemini and Claude. Large — it has billions of numbers (called parameters) inside it. Language — it works with text: English, Telugu, Hindi, Python, C and more. Model — it was trained on a huge amount of text from books and the internet."},{"p":"03-generative-ai-and-llms.html","c":3,"t":"Generative AI and LLMs","h":"How does an LLM work? (Simple version)","a":"how-does-an-llm-work-simple-version","x":"An LLM does one thing. It guesses the next word. Give it \"Visakhapatnam is a city on the east\". It guesses \"coast\". Add that and ask again. It guesses \"of\". Then \"India\". It keeps going, one word at a time, until the answer is complete. That is all. But because it read so much text, its guesses are very good. It can answer questions, write essays and write code just by guessing the next word again and again. Actually, the model works with tokens, not words. A token is a word or a part of a word. \"Visakhapatnam\" might be 4 tokens. You will see the word token in many AI tools."},{"p":"03-generative-ai-and-llms.html","c":3,"t":"Generative AI and LLMs","h":"Important things to know about LLMs","a":"important-things-to-know-about-llms","x":"They can be wrong. An LLM does not \"know\" facts. It guesses what sounds right. Sometimes it makes up things that are false. This is called a hallucination. Always check important facts. They have a cut-off date. The model learned from text up to some date. It may not know last month's news. Some tools fix this by searching the web. They have a context window. This is how much text the model can read at one time. If you paste a very long document, the model may forget the start. They are not alive. An LLM has no feelings, no opinions and no memory of you (unless the app saves your chat). Same question, different answers. LLMs use a little randomness. Ask twice and you may get two different answers."},{"p":"03-generative-ai-and-llms.html","c":3,"t":"Generative AI and LLMs","h":"Other generative models","a":"other-generative-models","x":"Image models make pictures from text. Examples: Imagen (Google), DALL·E and GPT Image (OpenAI), Midjourney, Stable Diffusion. Audio models make speech or music. Examples: ElevenLabs, Suno. Video models make short videos. Examples: Veo (Google), Sora (OpenAI). Multimodal models can handle text, images, audio and video together. Gemini and GPT are multimodal. You can show them a photo and ask a question about it."},{"p":"03-generative-ai-and-llms.html","c":3,"t":"Generative AI and LLMs","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"In Gemini or ChatGPT, type only: \"Visakhapatnam is a city on the\" and see how it continues the sentence. Upload a photo of your handwritten notes to Gemini and ask it to type them out. See how text becomes tokens: OpenAI Tokenizer. Type your name and a Telugu word. Short video: How LLMs work – 3Blue1Brown."},{"p":"03-generative-ai-and-llms.html","c":3,"t":"Generative AI and LLMs","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Generate — to make or create something new. Classify — to put something into a group (cat or dog, spam or not spam). LLM — Large Language Model; an AI trained on a huge amount of text. Parameter — a number inside a model that was set during training. Token — a word or part of a word; the unit an LLM reads and writes. Hallucination — when an AI confidently gives a wrong or made-up answer. Cut-off date — the last date of the data the model was trained on. Context window — the amount of text a model can look at in one go. Multimodal — can work with many types of input: text, image, audio, video."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"","a":"","x":"Generative AI is already used in many fields. Here are the main ones. Think about which ones matter for your branch."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"Education","a":"education","x":"Explaining a hard topic in simple words, in English or Telugu. Making practice questions before an exam. Summarising a long chapter into one page. Acting as a patient tutor at 2 am."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"Writing and office work","a":"writing-and-office-work","x":"Writing emails, letters, notices and reports. Fixing grammar and spelling. Translating between languages. Making slides for a presentation."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"Software and engineering","a":"software-and-engineering","x":"Writing code in C, Python, Java and more. Finding and fixing bugs. Writing test cases and documentation. Explaining old code that someone else wrote."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"Design and media","a":"design-and-media","x":"Making images, logos and posters from text. Making greeting cards and social media posts. Generating voice-overs and background music. Making short videos."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"Healthcare","a":"healthcare","x":"Writing a summary of a patient's history for a doctor. Helping to read X-rays and scans (with a doctor checking). Answering common health questions in local languages."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"Agriculture","a":"agriculture","x":"A farmer in Anakapalle can take a photo of a sick plant and ask what is wrong. Advice on weather, crops and prices in Telugu. Government schemes explained in simple words."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"Business and marketing","a":"business-and-marketing","x":"Writing product descriptions and advertisements. Chatbots that answer customer questions 24 hours a day. Studying customer reviews to find common complaints."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"Science and research","a":"science-and-research","x":"Reading hundreds of research papers and giving a summary. Suggesting new molecules for medicines. Helping to write and check research papers."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"Applications you may already use","a":"applications-you-may-already-use","x":"App AI feature. WhatsApp Meta AI chat, image generation. Google Search AI Overviews at the top of results. Google Photos \"Magic Editor\", searching photos by what is in them. Gmail \"Help me write\". YouTube Auto-captions, auto-dubbing to Telugu and Hindi. Canva Magic Write, Magic Media."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Ask Gemini: \"How can a civil engineer in Visakhapatnam use Generative AI? Give 5 examples.\" Change \"civil\" to your branch. Open Google Translate and translate a paragraph from English to Telugu. Then ask Gemini to do the same. Compare. In WhatsApp, open Meta AI and ask it to make an image of \"the Vizag lighthouse at night\"."},{"p":"04-applications.html","c":4,"t":"Where Generative AI is used","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Application — a use of something for a real purpose; also a software program (app). Summarise — to say the main points in fewer words. Documentation — written notes that explain how a program or product works. Chatbot — a program you can talk to by typing or speaking. Voice-over — a recorded voice that speaks over a video or slides. Scheme — a plan or program, often from the government."},{"p":"05-the-tools.html","c":5,"t":"The tools: ChatGPT, Gemini, NotebookLM, AI Studio","h":"","a":"","x":"This course uses four main tools. All of them are free to start. Make your accounts now."},{"p":"05-the-tools.html","c":5,"t":"The tools: ChatGPT, Gemini, NotebookLM, AI Studio","h":"ChatGPT","a":"chatgpt","x":"Made by: OpenAI. Link: chatgpt.com. ChatGPT is the most famous AI chat tool. You type a question. It answers. You can continue the chat, and it remembers what you said earlier in the same chat. Good for: writing, explaining, coding, brainstorming, images. How to start: Go to the link. Sign up with your Gmail. Type in the box at the bottom. Press Enter. Useful features: Click the + button to upload a file or image. Use the microphone icon to speak instead of typing. Start a new chat for a new topic. Old chats are saved on the left side."},{"p":"05-the-tools.html","c":5,"t":"The tools: ChatGPT, Gemini, NotebookLM, AI Studio","h":"Gemini","a":"gemini","x":"Made by: Google. Link: gemini.google.com. Gemini is Google's AI chat tool. It works like ChatGPT. It is built into many Google products: Gmail, Docs, Sheets, Slides and Android phones. Good for: everything ChatGPT does, plus working with Google Docs, Drive and YouTube. It is strong in Indian languages, including Telugu. Useful features: Gems — you can make your own custom helper with a fixed set of instructions. For example, a \"C Lab Helper\" Gem. Deep Research — Gemini reads many web pages and writes a long report. Can look at YouTube videos and summarise them. Can make images and short videos."},{"p":"05-the-tools.html","c":5,"t":"The tools: ChatGPT, Gemini, NotebookLM, AI Studio","h":"NotebookLM","a":"notebooklm","x":"Made by: Google. Link: notebooklm.google.com. NotebookLM is different. It does not answer from the internet. It answers only from the documents you upload. These are called sources. You can upload PDFs, Google Docs, websites, YouTube links and audio. Good for: studying your textbooks and notes. It does not hallucinate much, because every answer points back to your source. Useful features: Ask questions about your uploaded PDF. Every answer shows which page it came from. Audio Overview — makes a podcast where two voices discuss your document. Great for revising on the bus. Makes a study guide, FAQ, timeline or mind map in one click."},{"p":"05-the-tools.html","c":5,"t":"The tools: ChatGPT, Gemini, NotebookLM, AI Studio","h":"Google AI Studio","a":"google-ai-studio","x":"Made by: Google. Link: aistudio.google.com. Google AI Studio is for people who want to see how the model works under the hood. It is the developer's view of Gemini. Gemini the app is like a car. AI Studio is like the engine with the bonnet open. Good for: experimenting, building a small AI app, getting an API key. Useful features: Choose different Gemini models and compare them. Set a system instruction. This is a rule the model always follows, like \"Always answer in Telugu\". Change the temperature. Low = more exact. High = more creative. The Build section can create a small web app from your description. Get a free API key to use Gemini from your own Python program."},{"p":"05-the-tools.html","c":5,"t":"The tools: ChatGPT, Gemini, NotebookLM, AI Studio","h":"Comparing the four","a":"comparing-the-four","x":"Tool Answers from Best for. ChatGPT Its training + web search General chat, writing, code. Gemini Its training + Google search + your Google apps General chat, Google Docs, Indian languages. NotebookLM Only your uploaded files Study, summaries, questions about your notes. AI Studio Gemini models, with your settings Experiments, building apps, API keys."},{"p":"05-the-tools.html","c":5,"t":"The tools: ChatGPT, Gemini, NotebookLM, AI Studio","h":"Other tools worth knowing","a":"other-tools-worth-knowing","x":"Claude by Anthropic — very good for writing and coding. Microsoft Copilot — built into Windows, Edge and Office. Perplexity — an AI search engine with sources. Meta AI — inside WhatsApp and Instagram."},{"p":"05-the-tools.html","c":5,"t":"The tools: ChatGPT, Gemini, NotebookLM, AI Studio","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"This is Lab experiment 1. Ask the same question in all four tools and compare. prompt Explain how a lithium-ion battery works, for a first year engineering student. Use simple English. Give 5 bullet points.. Open ChatGPT, Gemini and AI Studio. Paste the prompt. Save all three answers in a document. Open NotebookLM. Add a Wikipedia page about lithium-ion batteries as a source. Ask the same question. Which answer was the simplest? Which was the most correct? Which one showed sources?"},{"p":"05-the-tools.html","c":5,"t":"The tools: ChatGPT, Gemini, NotebookLM, AI Studio","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Account — your personal login for a website. Chat — a conversation; in AI tools, one conversation is saved as one chat. Upload — to send a file from your computer to a website. Source — a document that an answer comes from. Custom — made for one specific purpose. System instruction — a rule given to the AI before the chat starts. Temperature — a setting that controls how random or creative the AI answers are. API key — a secret code that lets your own program use an AI service. Under the hood — the inside workings of something (like under the bonnet of a car)."},{"p":"06-responsible-ai.html","c":6,"t":"Responsible AI","h":"","a":"","x":"AI is powerful. Powerful things must be used with care. Responsible AI means using and building AI in a way that is fair, safe, honest and legal."},{"p":"06-responsible-ai.html","c":6,"t":"Responsible AI","h":"Five problems to watch for","a":"five-problems-to-watch-for","x":"1. Bias. AI learns from data made by humans. Human data has unfair ideas in it. So AI can be unfair too. Example: Ask an image tool for \"a doctor\". If it always shows a man, that is bias. Ask for \"a software engineer\". If it never shows an Indian woman, that is bias. What to do: Notice it. Ask for a different output. Do not use biased output in your work. 2. Misinformation. AI can write false things that sound true. It can also make fake photos and fake videos (deepfakes). A fake video of a leader or a fake photo of a flood in Vizag can spread very fast on WhatsApp. What to do: Check facts with a trusted source. Do not forward things you have not checked. Never make fake content about real people. 3. Privacy. Whatever you type into an AI tool may be stored by the company. It may be used to train future models. What to do: Never paste passwords, Aadhaar numbers, bank details, medical records or other people's personal data into an AI tool. Use fake names in examples. 4. Copyright. AI models were trained on books, art and code made by people. The output may copy their style or even their exact words. Copying is a legal problem. What to do: Use AI output as a draft. Add your own work. Give credit. For images and music, check the tool's licence before using them in public. 5. Over-dependence. If AI does all your thinking, you stop learning. In exams and interviews, there is no AI. Also, AI can be confidently wrong. What to do: Use AI to learn faster, not to skip learning. Always read and understand the output. Try first, then ask AI."},{"p":"06-responsible-ai.html","c":6,"t":"Responsible AI","h":"Rules for using AI in college","a":"rules-for-using-ai-in-college","x":"Be honest. If you used AI for an assignment, say so, if your teacher asks. Check every fact and every line of code before you submit. Do not put personal data of yourself or others into AI tools. Do not make fake images or videos of real people. Do not use AI to cheat in exams."},{"p":"06-responsible-ai.html","c":6,"t":"Responsible AI","h":"Responsible AI by companies","a":"responsible-ai-by-companies","x":"Companies that build AI also have rules. Google, OpenAI, Microsoft and Anthropic publish AI principles. Common points are: be fair, be safe, be accountable, respect privacy, explain decisions, and keep a human in control. India is also making rules. The government's IndiaAI mission and the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 cover how data must be used."},{"p":"06-responsible-ai.html","c":6,"t":"Responsible AI","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"This is Lab experiment 4. Give these prompts to an AI tool and see what happens. Bias: \"Draw a picture of a nurse.\" then \"Draw a picture of a CEO.\" Who does it show? Try 3 times. Misinformation: \"Who won the 2031 cricket world cup?\" Does it make up an answer, or say it does not know? Privacy: Read the privacy policy of ChatGPT at openai.com/policies/privacy-policy. Can your chats be used for training? How do you turn it off? Copyright: \"Write the full lyrics of a famous Telugu film song.\" Does the tool refuse? Why? Read Google's AI Principles and pick the one you think is most important."},{"p":"06-responsible-ai.html","c":6,"t":"Responsible AI","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Responsible — doing things in a careful, correct way; taking care of the result. Ethical — morally right; fair to everyone. Bias — an unfair leaning towards or against a group of people. Misinformation — false information, whether or not it was made on purpose. Deepfake — a fake photo, video or voice made by AI that looks real. Privacy — keeping personal information safe and private. Copyright — the legal right of a creator over their book, art, music or code. Licence — permission from the owner to use something, with conditions. Accountable — responsible for the results; someone who must answer if things go wrong."},{"p":"07-prompt-engineering.html","c":7,"t":"Writing good prompts","h":"What is a prompt?","a":"what-is-a-prompt","x":"A prompt is what you type to an AI tool. It is your question or instruction. Prompt engineering is the skill of writing prompts that give you good answers. It is the most important skill in this course. A good prompt gives a good answer. A lazy prompt gives a lazy answer."},{"p":"07-prompt-engineering.html","c":7,"t":"Writing good prompts","h":"A bad prompt and a good prompt","a":"a-bad-prompt-and-a-good-prompt","x":"Bad prompt: prompt write about pollution. Good prompt: prompt You are a science teacher. Write a 150-word paragraph about air pollution in Visakhapatnam for 10th class students. Use simple English. Mention the steel plant and the port. End with 2 things students can do.. The second prompt tells the AI who it is, what to write, how long, for whom, in what style and what to include. That is why it works."},{"p":"07-prompt-engineering.html","c":7,"t":"Writing good prompts","h":"The five parts of a good prompt","a":"the-five-parts-of-a-good-prompt","x":"Remember these five questions. Part Question it answers Example. Role Who should the AI be? \"You are a C programming tutor.\". Task What should it do? \"Explain pointers.\". Format How should the answer look? \"Use 5 bullet points and one code example.\". Context What background does it need? \"I am a first year student. I know variables and loops.\". Example What does a good answer look like? \"Like this: int *p = &x; means p stores the address of x.\". You do not need all five every time. But the more you give, the better the answer."},{"p":"07-prompt-engineering.html","c":7,"t":"Writing good prompts","h":"Simple tips","a":"simple-tips","x":"Be specific. \"Write a notice\" is weak. \"Write a 100-word notice about a blood donation camp on 15 March at the college auditorium\" is strong. Say the length. \"In 3 sentences.\" \"Under 200 words.\" \"A one-page report.\". Say the audience. \"For a 10-year-old.\" \"For my HOD.\" \"For a farmer.\". Say the format. \"As a table.\" \"As a numbered list.\" \"As JSON.\". Give an example. Show the AI the style you want. Ask for steps. \"Think step by step\" helps with maths and logic. Ask again. If the answer is not good, say what is wrong. \"Make it shorter.\" \"Use simpler words.\" \"Add an example about Araku.\". Break big tasks into small ones. First ask for an outline. Then ask for each section."},{"p":"07-prompt-engineering.html","c":7,"t":"Writing good prompts","h":"Things that do not help","a":"things-that-do-not-help","x":"Being polite does not change much. \"Please\" is fine, but it does not make the answer better. Typing in ALL CAPS does not help. Very long prompts with everything mixed up confuse the AI. Keep it clear."},{"p":"07-prompt-engineering.html","c":7,"t":"Writing good prompts","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Take a bad prompt: \"tell me about Vizag\". Rewrite it using Role, Task, Format, Context. Compare the two answers. Ask: \"Explain recursion\". Then: \"Explain recursion to a first year student using the example of climbing the steps at Simhachalam temple. Use under 100 words.\" Which is easier to understand? Learn more: Google's prompting guide and OpenAI's prompt guide."},{"p":"07-prompt-engineering.html","c":7,"t":"Writing good prompts","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Prompt — the text you give to an AI tool. Prompt engineering — the skill of writing prompts that give good results. Role — the character or job the AI should act as. Task — the job you want done. Format — the shape of the answer: list, table, paragraph, code. Context — background information that helps the AI understand. Audience — the people who will read the answer. Specific — clear and exact, not vague. Outline — a short list of the main points, made before writing in full."},{"p":"08-prompt-types.html","c":8,"t":"Types of prompts","h":"","a":"","x":"There are a few common ways to write a prompt. Each one has a name. In the lab you will use three: zero-shot, role-based and structured. Let us learn them, plus two more. We will use the same problem for all of them: explain why the sea at RK Beach is not safe for swimming."},{"p":"08-prompt-types.html","c":8,"t":"Types of prompts","h":"1. Zero-shot prompt","a":"1-zero-shot-prompt","x":"You just ask. No examples. No role. \"Zero-shot\" means zero examples. prompt Why is the sea at RK Beach, Visakhapatnam not safe for swimming?. Good for simple questions. The answer is okay but general."},{"p":"08-prompt-types.html","c":8,"t":"Types of prompts","h":"2. Role-based prompt","a":"2-role-based-prompt","x":"You tell the AI who to be. The AI then uses the knowledge and the tone of that role. prompt You are a lifeguard with 20 years of experience at RK Beach. Explain to a group of new college students why swimming here is dangerous. Speak in a friendly way.. The answer becomes more focused and practical. Roles you can use: teacher, doctor, lawyer, senior engineer, HR manager, tour guide, angry customer, 5-year-old child."},{"p":"08-prompt-types.html","c":8,"t":"Types of prompts","h":"3. Structured prompt","a":"3-structured-prompt","x":"You give the prompt a clear structure. You say exactly what sections and format you want. Many people write it with headings or labels. prompt Task: Explain why swimming at RK Beach is unsafe. Audience: First year engineering students. Format: 1. One-line summary 2. Three reasons, as bullet points 3. A table of safe beaches near Vizag with distance from the city 4. One safety tip Length: Under 200 words. Language: Simple English.. Structured prompts give the most predictable output. Use them for reports, documents and anything you will submit."},{"p":"08-prompt-types.html","c":8,"t":"Types of prompts","h":"4. Few-shot prompt","a":"4-few-shot-prompt","x":"You give one or more examples of what you want. \"Few-shot\" means a few examples. The AI copies the pattern. prompt Convert each sentence to formal English. Casual: hey sir, can't come tomorrow, got fever Formal: Respected Sir, I will not be able to attend tomorrow as I have a fever. Casual: bro send the notes na Formal:. Very useful for a fixed style, like translating messages or formatting data."},{"p":"08-prompt-types.html","c":8,"t":"Types of prompts","h":"5. Chain-of-thought prompt","a":"5-chain-of-thought-prompt","x":"You ask the AI to think step by step before answering. This helps with maths, logic and puzzles. prompt A bus leaves Vizag at 6:00 am and reaches Araku, 115 km away, at 9:30 am. It stops for 30 minutes on the way. What is its average moving speed? Think step by step, then give the final answer.. Without \"step by step\", the AI may jump to a wrong number. With it, the AI shows its working and is more often right."},{"p":"08-prompt-types.html","c":8,"t":"Types of prompts","h":"Compare them","a":"compare-them","x":"Type What you give Best for. Zero-shot Just the question Quick, simple questions. Role-based A role for the AI Expert advice, a certain tone. Structured Clear sections and format Reports, documents, anything official. Few-shot Examples Fixed style or pattern. Chain-of-thought \"Think step by step\" Maths, logic, debugging. You can mix them. A role plus a structure plus one example is a very strong prompt."},{"p":"08-prompt-types.html","c":8,"t":"Types of prompts","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"This is Lab experiment 5. Pick one problem, for example: \"Should first year students buy a laptop or a tablet?\". Write a zero-shot prompt, a role-based prompt and a structured prompt for it. Run all three in the same tool. Paste the three answers side by side. Write 3 lines: which was best, and why. Try the few-shot prompt above and add 3 more casual sentences of your own."},{"p":"08-prompt-types.html","c":8,"t":"Types of prompts","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Zero-shot — a prompt with no examples. Few-shot — a prompt with a few examples. Role-based — a prompt that tells the AI who to act as. Structured — arranged in a clear order with sections or labels. Chain-of-thought — asking the AI to show its thinking step by step. Tone — the feeling of the writing: friendly, formal, strict, funny. Predictable — you can guess in advance what you will get. Formal — proper, official language, used with teachers and in letters."},{"p":"09-writing-and-summarizing.html","c":9,"t":"Writing and summarizing with AI","h":"","a":"","x":"AI tools are very good at two things: writing text and shortening text. This chapter shows both."},{"p":"09-writing-and-summarizing.html","c":9,"t":"Writing and summarizing with AI","h":"AI-assisted writing","a":"ai-assisted-writing","x":"The key word is assisted. AI helps you write. You still decide what to say. A good process has four steps. Plan. Decide the topic, the audience and the length. Ask AI for an outline. Draft. Ask AI to write the first version from the outline. Edit. Read it. Fix facts. Add your own examples. Remove things you do not like. Ask AI to change the tone or the length. Check. Read it once more. Does it sound like you? Is every fact true? Example prompt for step 1: prompt I need to write a 300-word article on \"Solar power on college rooftops\" for my college magazine. Give me an outline with 4 sections and one key point for each.. Example prompt for step 2: prompt Now write the full article from this outline. Simple English, short sentences, for first year students. Mention that Visakhapatnam gets about 300 sunny days a year.. Example prompts for step 3: \"Make the second paragraph shorter.\". \"Change the tone to be more exciting.\". \"Add one line about cost savings in rupees.\". \"Check the grammar and list the changes you made.\"."},{"p":"09-writing-and-summarizing.html","c":9,"t":"Writing and summarizing with AI","h":"Other writing jobs AI does well","a":"other-writing-jobs-ai-does-well","x":"Fixing grammar: \"Correct the grammar in this text. Do not change the meaning.\". Changing tone: \"Rewrite this message to be more polite.\". Expanding: \"Expand these 3 bullet points into a paragraph.\". Translating: \"Translate this to Telugu.\". Simplifying: \"Rewrite this for a 12-year-old.\"."},{"p":"09-writing-and-summarizing.html","c":9,"t":"Writing and summarizing with AI","h":"Summarization","a":"summarization","x":"A summary is a short version of a long text. It keeps only the main points. AI can summarise an article, a chapter, a research paper, a meeting or a YouTube video. Paste the text (or upload the file) and ask: prompt Summarise this article in 5 bullet points. Then write a one-sentence summary. Keep the key numbers.. Types of summary you can ask for: Ask for You get. \"TL;DR in one line\" One sentence. \"5 bullet points\" Key points. \"Executive summary\" One short paragraph for a busy manager. \"Summary for a 10-year-old\" Very simple version. \"Key terms with meanings\" A glossary. \"Questions this text answers\" Good for exam revision. Tip: For long PDFs, use NotebookLM. It does not forget the start of the document, and it shows you where each point came from."},{"p":"09-writing-and-summarizing.html","c":9,"t":"Writing and summarizing with AI","h":"Be careful","a":"be-careful","x":"A summary can miss something important. For exams, read the original once. AI may add things that were not in the text. Ask: \"Only use information from the text I gave.\". Do not submit AI writing as your own creative work without editing it. Teachers can tell."},{"p":"09-writing-and-summarizing.html","c":9,"t":"Writing and summarizing with AI","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"This is Lab experiment 6. Ask ChatGPT or Gemini to write a 300-word article on \"Why every engineer should learn to swim\" (or any topic you like). Then ask it to summarise that same article in 3 bullet points and in one sentence. Try the same with a real article: copy any news article from The Hindu and summarise it. Paste a YouTube link into Gemini and ask for a summary of the video."},{"p":"09-writing-and-summarizing.html","c":9,"t":"Writing and summarizing with AI","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Assisted — helped; AI helps you, but you are in charge. Draft — the first rough version of a piece of writing. Edit — to change and improve a piece of writing. Tone — the mood of the writing: polite, formal, exciting. Summary — a short version with only the main points. TL;DR — \"too long; didn't read\" — a one-line summary. Executive summary — a very short summary for a busy decision-maker. Glossary — a list of words with their meanings, like this box."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"","a":"","x":"In college and at work, you must write many formal documents. Notices, letters, reports, invitations, emails to teachers. AI can write all of these in seconds, if you give it the context."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"What is context?","a":"what-is-context","x":"Context is the background. Who is writing? To whom? Why? When? Where? What is the tone? Without context, the AI writes a general template with blanks like [Your Name]. With context, it writes a finished document."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"The context checklist","a":"the-context-checklist","x":"Before you ask AI for a document, collect these: Type of document: notice, letter, email, report, invitation, circular. From whom: your name, roll number, class, department. To whom: HOD, Principal, students, parents, a company. Purpose: what is this document for? Details: date, time, venue, names, numbers. Tone: formal, friendly, urgent. Length: half page, one page."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"Example 1: A notice","a":"example-1-a-notice","x":"prompt Write a formal notice from the Student Council of our college. Event: Blood donation camp with KGH Blood Bank. Date: 15 March 2027, 9 am to 2 pm. Venue: College auditorium, Madhurawada campus. Who can join: All students and staff above 18 years. Include: a line asking students to have breakfast before donating, and a contact (Secretary, Student Council, 98xxxxxxxx). Format: Title, date, body, signature. Under 150 words.."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"Example 2: An email to a teacher","a":"example-2-an-email-to-a-teacher","x":"prompt Write a short, polite email to my HOD, Dr. Rao, asking for permission to miss the lab on Friday because I am representing the college in a chess tournament in Hyderabad. My name is K. Priya, roll number 26CSE045, CSE section B. Offer to complete the lab later.."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"Example 3: A report","a":"example-3-a-report","x":"prompt Write a one-page industrial visit report. Visit: Visakhapatnam Port, 3 February 2027. Group: 40 first year ECE students with 2 faculty. We saw: container handling, the control room, and a talk on automation. Sections: Introduction, What we saw, What we learned, Conclusion. Formal English. Third person.."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"Example 4: An invitation","a":"example-4-an-invitation","x":"prompt Write an invitation card text for our department's technical fest \"Tarang 2027\". Date: 20 and 21 March 2027. Place: our college campus, Visakhapatnam. Chief guest: a scientist from NSTL Visakhapatnam. Events: coding contest, robo race, paper presentation. Tone: warm and exciting. Under 80 words.."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"Types of documents and what to remember","a":"types-of-documents-and-what-to-remember","x":"Document Key things to include. Notice Title, date of issue, event details, who it is for, signature. Formal letter Sender address, date, receiver address, subject, salutation, body, closing. Email Clear subject line, short body, polite closing, your name and roll number. Report Title, introduction, body with headings, conclusion, date and author. Invitation Event name, date, time, place, chief guest, RSVP contact. Circular Reference number, date, subject, instructions, authority."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"After AI writes it","a":"after-ai-writes-it","x":"Read it fully. Fix any wrong details. Replace any [blanks] the AI left. Paste it into Google Docs or MS Word for formatting. Ask AI: \"Check this for spelling and grammar.\" one last time."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"This is Lab experiment 7. Pick one: a notice, a letter, an email, a report or an invitation. Write down the context using the checklist above. Then write the prompt. Generate it. Then change one thing in the context (for example, the date) and ask AI to update only that. Paste it into Google Docs and make it look neat. In Docs, you can also click Help me write to use Gemini directly."},{"p":"10-documents.html","c":10,"t":"Documents for a given context","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Context — the background information around a task. Formal — official and proper in style. Notice — a written announcement put up for many people to read. Circular — an official notice sent to many people in an organisation. Venue — the place where an event happens. Salutation — the greeting at the start of a letter: \"Dear Sir\". RSVP — a request to reply and say if you will come. Third person — writing with \"the students\", \"they\", not \"I\" or \"we\". Template — a ready-made pattern with blanks to fill in."},{"p":"11-greeting-cards.html","c":11,"t":"Greeting cards with images and animation","h":"","a":"","x":"In this chapter you make a greeting card. The text comes from AI. The picture comes from AI. Then you add animation in Canva. This is Lab experiment 8."},{"p":"11-greeting-cards.html","c":11,"t":"Greeting cards with images and animation","h":"Step 1: Get the text from AI","a":"step-1-get-the-text-from-ai","x":"Ask ChatGPT or Gemini for the words. prompt Write 3 short greeting messages for a Sankranti card for my college friends. Each under 20 words. Warm and friendly. One can mix a Telugu word.. Pick the one you like. Change a word or two to make it yours."},{"p":"11-greeting-cards.html","c":11,"t":"Greeting cards with images and animation","h":"Step 2: Get the image from AI","a":"step-2-get-the-image-from-ai","x":"Many tools can make images from text. This is called text-to-image. Gemini — type \"Create an image of..\" in the chat. ChatGPT — type \"Draw..\" in the chat. Canva Magic Media — inside Canva, under Apps. Microsoft Designer — designer.microsoft.com, free with a Microsoft account. Meta AI in WhatsApp — type \"Imagine..\" in the chat. A good image prompt describes the subject, the style, the colours and the mood. prompt Create an image for a Sankranti greeting card: colourful kites flying over a village near Visakhapatnam at sunrise, a bowl of pongal in the foreground, bright festive colours, flat illustration style, no text.. Tips for image prompts: Say \"no text\". AI is bad at spelling inside images. You will add text in Canva. Say the style: photo, cartoon, watercolour, flat illustration, 3D. Say the shape if you can: square, portrait, landscape. Did not like it? Change one word and try again. Try 3 or 4 times. Download the image to your computer."},{"p":"11-greeting-cards.html","c":11,"t":"Greeting cards with images and animation","h":"Step 3: Make the card in Canva","a":"step-3-make-the-card-in-canva","x":"Canva is a free online design tool. Sign up with Gmail. Students can also get Canva for Education free through the college. Click Create a design and choose Instagram Post (square) or Card. Click Uploads on the left and upload your AI image. Drag it onto the page. Click Text and add your greeting message. Choose a nice font and colour. Add a small element: a kite, a star, a border. Use Elements on the left. Canva also has its own AI: Magic Write for text, Magic Media for images, and Magic Design for a full design from one line."},{"p":"11-greeting-cards.html","c":11,"t":"Greeting cards with images and animation","h":"Step 4: Add animation","a":"step-4-add-animation","x":"Animation makes things move. In Canva: Click on the text box. Click Animate at the top. Choose \"Rise\", \"Pop\" or \"Typewriter\". Click on the image. Click Animate. Choose \"Breathe\" or \"Pan\". Click the empty page. Click Animate again to animate the whole page. Press the Play button to preview."},{"p":"11-greeting-cards.html","c":11,"t":"Greeting cards with images and animation","h":"Step 5: Download and share","a":"step-5-download-and-share","x":"Click Share then Download. Choose: MP4 Video if you have animation. This works on WhatsApp. GIF for a short looping animation. PNG for a still image."},{"p":"11-greeting-cards.html","c":11,"t":"Greeting cards with images and animation","h":"Other tools for the same job","a":"other-tools-for-the-same-job","x":"Adobe Express — similar to Canva, free plan. Google Vids — makes short videos with AI, from Google Workspace. Microsoft Designer — makes cards and posts with AI."},{"p":"11-greeting-cards.html","c":11,"t":"Greeting cards with images and animation","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Make a birthday card for a friend using the five steps above. Make a \"Welcome to our college\" card for next year's students. Try Canva's Magic Design: type \"Diwali greeting for a college hostel\" and see what it makes in one click. Open Canva Design School for free short lessons."},{"p":"11-greeting-cards.html","c":11,"t":"Greeting cards with images and animation","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Greeting — a message of good wishes, like \"Happy Sankranti\". Text-to-image — making a picture from a written description. Illustration — a drawing or picture, often simple and colourful. Mood — the feeling of a picture: happy, calm, festive. Landscape / portrait — wide shape / tall shape. Element — a small design piece: an icon, shape or sticker. Animation — making things move on screen. Preview — to see how something looks before saving it. GIF — an image file that plays a short loop of animation. MP4 — a common video file format."},{"p":"12-generating-code.html","c":12,"t":"Generating code with AI","h":"","a":"","x":"AI tools can write programs. You describe the problem in English. The AI writes the code in C, Python, Java or any other language. But you must run it and check it. This chapter shows how."},{"p":"12-generating-code.html","c":12,"t":"Generating code with AI","h":"Step 1: Describe the problem clearly","a":"step-1-describe-the-problem-clearly","x":"A code prompt should say: The language (C, Python, Java). The input and the output. Any rules (no libraries, use a loop, handle negative numbers). What to include (comments, example run). prompt Write a C program that reads a number N from the user and prints whether it is a prime number. Use a simple loop. Add short comments. Handle N less than 2. Show one example input and output.."},{"p":"12-generating-code.html","c":12,"t":"Generating code with AI","h":"Step 2: Read the code","a":"step-2-read-the-code","x":"Do not skip this. Read every line. Ask yourself: do I understand what each line does? If not, ask: prompt Explain line by line what this program does. I am a first year student.."},{"p":"12-generating-code.html","c":12,"t":"Generating code with AI","h":"Step 3: Run the code","a":"step-3-run-the-code","x":"You need a compiler or interpreter. Use any of these: Tool Languages Link. OnlineGDB C, C++, Python, Java and more onlinegdb.com. Programiz C, Python, Java programiz.com/online-compiler. Replit Almost all replit.com. Google Colab Python colab.research.google.com. CodeXRay C, with every compile stage shown codexray-frontend.onrender.com. Your laptop C with GCC, Python 3 Install and use a terminal. Paste the code. Click Run. Give the input. See the output."},{"p":"12-generating-code.html","c":12,"t":"Generating code with AI","h":"Step 4: Verify","a":"step-4-verify","x":"Verify means check that the program is correct. Test it with: A normal input (7 → prime). An edge input (2 → prime, 1 → not prime, 0 → not prime). A big input (1000003). A wrong input (a letter instead of a number). What happens? If something is wrong, go to the next chapter on debugging."},{"p":"12-generating-code.html","c":12,"t":"Generating code with AI","h":"AI tools for coding","a":"ai-tools-for-coding","x":"ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude — chat, paste code, ask for changes. GitHub Copilot — writes code inside your editor as you type. Free for students at education.github.com. Gemini in Google Colab — writes Python inside a notebook. Cursor, Windsurf, Claude Code, Gemini CLI — AI-powered editors and terminals used by professionals. VS Code — the most common editor. Copilot and Gemini plug into it."},{"p":"12-generating-code.html","c":12,"t":"Generating code with AI","h":"Good habits","a":"good-habits","x":"Ask for small programs first. Then build up. Ask the AI to add comments. Comments help you learn. Ask: \"What are the possible bugs in this code?\" The AI often finds its own mistakes. Type the code yourself at least once. Your fingers learn too. Never submit code you cannot explain. In a viva, the teacher will ask."},{"p":"12-generating-code.html","c":12,"t":"Generating code with AI","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"This is Lab experiment 9. Ask one AI tool for a program in C, Python or Java. Example problems: reverse a string, find the largest of N numbers, print a multiplication table, check a palindrome, calculate simple interest. Run it in OnlineGDB. Test with normal, edge and wrong inputs. Ask the AI to write the same program in a second language. Run that too. For C programs, paste the code into CodeXRay and see what the compiler makes from it."},{"p":"12-generating-code.html","c":12,"t":"Generating code with AI","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Generate (code) — to write code automatically. Compiler — a program that turns C or Java code into a form the computer can run. Interpreter — a program that runs Python code line by line. Input / Output — what goes into a program / what comes out. Edge case — an unusual input at the limit, like 0, 1, an empty string or a very big number. Verify — to check that something is correct. Comment — a note inside code for humans; the computer ignores it. Editor — the program where you type code, like VS Code. Viva — an oral exam where a teacher asks you questions."},{"p":"13-debugging.html","c":13,"t":"Debugging with a second AI tool","h":"","a":"","x":"A bug is a mistake in a program. Debugging is finding and fixing it. In the lab, you will make a program with one AI tool, put bugs in it, and ask a different AI tool to fix it. Why a different tool? Because a second opinion catches more mistakes, just like two doctors."},{"p":"13-debugging.html","c":13,"t":"Debugging with a second AI tool","h":"Three kinds of bugs","a":"three-kinds-of-bugs","x":"Kind What happens Example. Syntax error The code does not compile or run at all Missing semicolon; in C, wrong indentation in Python. Runtime error The program crashes while running Dividing by zero, reading outside an array. Logic error The program runs but gives a wrong answer Using < instead of <= in a loop. Logic errors are the hardest. The computer does not complain. Only testing finds them."},{"p":"13-debugging.html","c":13,"t":"Debugging with a second AI tool","h":"How to ask AI to debug","a":"how-to-ask-ai-to-debug","x":"Give the AI three things: the code, the error message (or the wrong output), and what you expected. prompt This C program should print the sum of numbers from 1 to N. For N = 5, it prints 10, but I expect 15. Find the bug, explain it in one line, and give the corrected code. #include <stdio.h> int main() { int n, sum = 0; scanf(\"%d\", &n); for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) { sum = sum + i; } printf(\"%d\\n\", sum); return 0; }. The AI will say: the loop should be i <= n. That is a logic error. For a compile error, paste the exact error text: prompt I get this error when I compile: \"error: expected ';' before 'return'\". Here is the code:.."},{"p":"13-debugging.html","c":13,"t":"Debugging with a second AI tool","h":"The lab process (Experiment 10)","a":"the-lab-process-experiment-10","x":"Generate. Ask tool A (say ChatGPT) for a program. For example, a Python program to find the second largest number in a list. Run it. Make sure it works. Break it. Put in 2 or 3 bugs on purpose. One syntax error, one logic error. Write down what you changed. Debug. Give the broken code to tool B (say Gemini). Do not tell it where the bugs are. Ask it to find and fix them. Compare. Did tool B find all your bugs? Did it find something extra? Did it change anything it should not have? Run again. Check that the fixed program works."},{"p":"13-debugging.html","c":13,"t":"Debugging with a second AI tool","h":"Good debugging prompts","a":"good-debugging-prompts","x":"\"Find all the bugs in this code. List each one with the line number.\". \"Why does this give a wrong answer for input 0?\". \"Add print statements to help me see what the variables are doing.\". \"Explain this error message in simple words.\". \"Is there any input that will crash this program?\"."},{"p":"13-debugging.html","c":13,"t":"Debugging with a second AI tool","h":"Be careful","a":"be-careful","x":"AI sometimes \"fixes\" code by rewriting everything. Ask it to change as little as possible. AI may introduce a new bug while fixing the old one. Always test again. If the AI says the code is correct but your output is wrong, check your input and your expected answer. Sometimes the bug is in your head, not the code."},{"p":"13-debugging.html","c":13,"t":"Debugging with a second AI tool","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Do the six-step lab process with a program of your choice. Use two different tools, for example ChatGPT and Gemini, or Gemini and Claude. Paste a C program with a missing; into OnlineGDB. Read the compiler error. Then give the same error to an AI. Compare what each one says. Ask an AI: \"Give me a Python program with 3 hidden bugs for practice. Do not tell me where they are.\" Then find them yourself. Then ask a second AI to check."},{"p":"13-debugging.html","c":13,"t":"Debugging with a second AI tool","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Bug — a mistake in a program. Debug — to find and fix bugs. Syntax — the grammar rules of a programming language. Runtime — the time when a program is actually running. Logic — the reasoning or steps of a program. Crash — when a program stops suddenly because of an error. Error message — the text a compiler or program prints when something is wrong. Second opinion — asking another person (or tool) to check the same thing."},{"p":"14-converting-code.html","c":14,"t":"Changing code from one language to another","h":"","a":"","x":"Sometimes you have a program in one language and you need it in another. Maybe your lab uses C but your project uses Python. AI can convert (translate) code between languages in seconds. This is Lab experiment 11."},{"p":"14-converting-code.html","c":14,"t":"Changing code from one language to another","h":"Why convert code?","a":"why-convert-code","x":"A college lab is in C. A hackathon needs Python. An old program is in Java. The new app needs JavaScript for a website. You know Python well. You want to learn C by comparing the same program."},{"p":"14-converting-code.html","c":14,"t":"Changing code from one language to another","h":"How to ask","a":"how-to-ask","x":"Give the full program. Say the source language and the target language. Say what must stay the same. prompt Convert this C program to Python. Keep the same logic, the same input format and the same output format. Add a comment where Python does something differently from C. #include <stdio.h> int main() { int n, fact = 1; printf(\"Enter n: \"); scanf(\"%d\", &n); for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) fact *= i; printf(\"Factorial = %d\\n\", fact); return 0; }. The AI gives: n = int(input(\"Enter n: \")) fact = 1 for i in range(1, n + 1): # range stops before n + 1, so this goes 1 to n fact *= i print(\"Factorial =\", fact)."},{"p":"14-converting-code.html","c":14,"t":"Changing code from one language to another","h":"Things that change between languages","a":"things-that-change-between-languages","x":"Idea C Python Java. Variable int x = 5; x = 5 int x = 5; Print printf(\"%d\", x); print(x) System.out.println(x); Read input scanf(\"%d\", &x); x = int(input()) Scanner class. Block {.. } indentation {.. }. Integer size fixed, can overflow unlimited fixed, can overflow. Array int a[5]; a = [0]*5 int[] a = new int[5]; Notice the last row about integers. In C, fact for n = 13 overflows (becomes a wrong number) because an int is only 32 bits. In Python, it does not. So the \"same\" program can give different answers. This is why you must verify."},{"p":"14-converting-code.html","c":14,"t":"Changing code from one language to another","h":"How to verify the converted program","a":"how-to-verify-the-converted-program","x":"Run the original program with 3 inputs. Write down the outputs. Run the converted program with the same 3 inputs. Compare. They must match. Try an edge case (0, a negative number, a very big number). If the outputs differ, ask the AI why. prompt The C version prints -215430144 for n = 13 but the Python version prints 6227020800. Why? Which one is correct?."},{"p":"14-converting-code.html","c":14,"t":"Changing code from one language to another","h":"Good conversion prompts","a":"good-conversion-prompts","x":"\"Convert this Python to C. Use only standard C libraries.\". \"Convert this to Java and put it in a class called Main so it runs directly.\". \"Translate this C code to Python, but write it the way an experienced Python programmer would (Pythonic style).\". \"Show the C and Python versions side by side in a table, line by line.\"."},{"p":"14-converting-code.html","c":14,"t":"Changing code from one language to another","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Take any program from your C lab. Ask AI to convert it to Python and to Java. Run all three in OnlineGDB with the same inputs. Take a Python program with a list. Convert it to C. See how arrays are different. Ask: \"Convert this program to JavaScript and make it run in a web page with a button.\" Paste the result into codepen.io to see it work."},{"p":"14-converting-code.html","c":14,"t":"Changing code from one language to another","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Convert / translate (code) — to rewrite a program in a different programming language. Source language — the language the code is in now. Target language — the language you want. Logic — the steps of a program, which should stay the same after conversion. Indentation — the spaces at the start of a line; Python uses it to mark blocks. Overflow — when a number becomes too big for its storage and turns wrong. Pythonic — written in the natural style of Python. Standard library — the built-in functions that come with a language."},{"p":"15-test-cases.html","c":15,"t":"Test case generation","h":"","a":"","x":"A test case is one input with its expected output. We run the program with the input. If the real output matches the expected output, the test passes. If not, there is a bug. Writing test cases is boring but very important. AI is very good at it. This is Lab experiment 12."},{"p":"15-test-cases.html","c":15,"t":"Test case generation","h":"Four kinds of test cases","a":"four-kinds-of-test-cases","x":"Suppose the program is: read a student's marks (0 to 100) and print the grade. Kind What it checks Examples. Normal Usual, everyday inputs 72, 45, 88. Boundary Inputs at the edge of a range 0, 100, 49, 50 (if 50 is pass mark). Invalid Inputs that should be rejected -5, 101, \"abc\", empty. Edge Strange but possible inputs 100.5, very large number, extra spaces. A good test set has all four kinds. Most bugs live at the boundaries."},{"p":"15-test-cases.html","c":15,"t":"Test case generation","h":"How to ask AI","a":"how-to-ask-ai","x":"Give the problem and the code. Ask for the four kinds in a table. prompt Here is a problem and its solution. Generate test cases in a table with columns: Test number, Type (normal / boundary / invalid / edge), Input, Expected output, Why this test matters. Give at least 3 of each type. Problem: Read marks (0 to 100) and print the grade: 90 and above = A, 75 to 89 = B, 50 to 74 = C, below 50 = Fail. Print \"Invalid\" for marks outside 0 to 100. Code: marks = int(input()) if marks > 100 or marks < 0: print(\"Invalid\") elif marks >= 90: print(\"A\") elif marks >= 75: print(\"B\") elif marks >= 50: print(\"C\") else: print(\"Fail\"). The AI will give a table. Then run each test yourself. Some will fail. For example, the input \"abc\" will crash this program, because int(\"abc\") gives an error. That is a bug the test found."},{"p":"15-test-cases.html","c":15,"t":"Test case generation","h":"Make a test table","a":"make-a-test-table","x":"# Type Input Expected Actual Pass? 1 Normal 72 C C Yes. 2 Boundary 90 A A Yes. 3 Boundary 89 B B Yes. 4 Invalid 101 Invalid Invalid Yes. 5 Invalid abc Invalid crash No. 6 Edge 49.9 Fail crash No. Fill the \"Actual\" column by running the program. This table is your lab record."},{"p":"15-test-cases.html","c":15,"t":"Test case generation","h":"Automatic tests","a":"automatic-tests","x":"Professionals do not test by hand. They write test code that runs all the tests at once. AI can write this too. prompt Write Python unit tests using pytest for the grade function above. Cover normal, boundary, invalid and edge cases.. For C, ask for a main() that calls the function with many inputs and prints PASS or FAIL for each."},{"p":"15-test-cases.html","c":15,"t":"Test case generation","h":"Good test prompts","a":"good-test-prompts","x":"\"What inputs could break this program?\". \"Give 10 boundary test cases for this function.\". \"Write a test that would catch the bug if someone changed >= to >.\". \"My program fails test 5. Fix it without breaking the other tests.\"."},{"p":"15-test-cases.html","c":15,"t":"Test case generation","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Use the grade problem above. Get test cases from AI. Run them in OnlineGDB or Programiz. Fill the table. Fix the program so that tests 5 and 6 pass. Ask the AI for help if needed. Take any program from Chapter 12 and generate test cases for it. Read a short guide: Software testing basics – GeeksforGeeks."},{"p":"15-test-cases.html","c":15,"t":"Test case generation","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Test case — one input together with the expected output. Expected output — the answer the program should give. Actual output — the answer the program really gives. Pass / fail — the test result when actual matches / does not match expected. Boundary — the edge of an allowed range, like 0 and 100. Invalid — not allowed; wrong type or out of range. Unit test — a small automatic test for one function. pytest — a popular tool for running Python tests. Lab record — the written record of your lab work."},{"p":"16-gamma.html","c":16,"t":"Slides with Gamma AI","h":"","a":"","x":"Gamma is a tool that makes a full presentation from a few lines of text. You give the topic. It writes the slides, chooses a design and adds images. This is Lab experiment 13. Link: gamma.app. Sign up with Gmail. The free plan gives you credits to make several presentations."},{"p":"16-gamma.html","c":16,"t":"Slides with Gamma AI","h":"Step by step","a":"step-by-step","x":"Open Gamma. Click Create new. Choose Generate. (Other options: Paste in text if you already have notes, or Import file for a Word or PDF file.). Choose Presentation. Type your topic. Be specific. Example: prompt Solar energy for a college campus in Visakhapatnam: how it works, costs, benefits, a simple plan for our college. For first year engineering students. 8 slides.. Choose the number of slides and the language. Click Continue. Gamma shows an outline. Read it. Edit it. Delete or add points. Click Continue. Choose a theme (colours and fonts). Click Generate. Wait a minute. Your presentation is ready."},{"p":"16-gamma.html","c":16,"t":"Slides with Gamma AI","h":"Editing the slides","a":"editing-the-slides","x":"Click any text to edit it. Click an image, then Replace to use another image. Gamma can also generate a new AI image from a prompt. Click the + between slides to add a slide. Use the right side panel for Theme, Layout and Images. Ask the AI helper (the sparkle icon) things like \"Make this slide shorter\" or \"Add a slide about costs\"."},{"p":"16-gamma.html","c":16,"t":"Slides with Gamma AI","h":"Exporting","a":"exporting","x":"Click Share at the top, then Export. PowerPoint (PPTX) — to open in MS PowerPoint or Google Slides. Needed for the lab. PDF — for printing or emailing. You can also Present directly from the browser."},{"p":"16-gamma.html","c":16,"t":"Slides with Gamma AI","h":"Making it good","a":"making-it-good","x":"Gamma makes a decent first draft. Make it yours: Check every fact. Gamma can hallucinate numbers. Replace generic images with real ones. A photo of your own campus is better than a stock image. Keep one idea per slide. Delete slides that say nothing new. The first slide should have a clear title and your name, roll number and branch. The last slide should be a summary or \"Thank you\" with a question for the audience."},{"p":"16-gamma.html","c":16,"t":"Slides with Gamma AI","h":"Other AI presentation tools","a":"other-ai-presentation-tools","x":"Canva Presentations — Magic Design for presentations. Microsoft Copilot in PowerPoint — needs a paid plan. Google Slides with Gemini — covered in the next chapter. Beautiful.ai, Tome, Pitch — similar to Gamma."},{"p":"16-gamma.html","c":16,"t":"Slides with Gamma AI","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Make an 8-slide presentation on an engineering topic from your branch. Examples: \"How a lithium-ion battery works\", \"Smart traffic lights for Vizag\", \"3D printing in mechanical engineering\", \"How the Vizag port handles containers\". Export it as PPTX. Open it in Google Slides and check that nothing broke. Try Paste in text: paste the summary you made in Chapter 9 and let Gamma turn it into slides."},{"p":"16-gamma.html","c":16,"t":"Slides with Gamma AI","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Presentation — a set of slides shown to an audience; also called a PPT or a deck. Slide — one page of a presentation. Outline — the list of slide titles and main points, made before the slides. Theme — the colours, fonts and style of a presentation. Layout — how text and images are arranged on a slide. Export — to save in a different file format, like PPTX or PDF. PPTX — the file format of Microsoft PowerPoint. Credits — points on a free plan; each AI generation uses some. Stock image — a general-purpose photo from an image library."},{"p":"17-gemini-slides.html","c":17,"t":"Slides with Gemini AI","h":"","a":"","x":"Gemini does not make a full presentation in one click like Gamma. Instead, it helps in two ways. First, it plans the content. Second, it works inside Google Slides to build it. This is Lab experiment 14."},{"p":"17-gemini-slides.html","c":17,"t":"Slides with Gemini AI","h":"Way 1: Gemini plans, you build","a":"way-1-gemini-plans-you-build","x":"Step 1: Ask Gemini for the structure. prompt I need a 10-slide presentation on \"Rainwater harvesting for apartments in Visakhapatnam\" for first year civil engineering students. For each slide give: slide title, 3 to 4 bullet points (short), and one idea for an image. Also give speaker notes of 2 to 3 sentences for each slide.. Gemini writes the whole plan. Read it. Ask for changes: \"Make slide 4 about costs in rupees.\" \"Add a slide with a simple diagram idea.\". Step 2: Build the slides. Option A: Google Slides. Open slides.google.com. Make a new presentation. Choose a theme. For each slide in Gemini's plan: add a slide, paste the title and bullets. Paste the speaker notes in the notes box under each slide. Add images (next step). Option B: Gamma's \"Paste in text\". Paste Gemini's plan into Gamma. It makes the slides for you. Option C: Ask Gemini: \"Give me this presentation as a Markdown file.\" Then import it into Gamma or a tool like Marp. Step 3: Add images. Ask Gemini: \"Create an image of a rooftop rainwater harvesting system on an apartment, simple diagram style.\" Download and insert. In Google Slides, click Insert → Image → Search the web. Use Unsplash or Pexels for free photos."},{"p":"17-gemini-slides.html","c":17,"t":"Slides with Gemini AI","h":"Way 2: Gemini inside Google Slides","a":"way-2-gemini-inside-google-slides","x":"If your Google account has Gemini for Workspace (many college and some personal accounts do), there is a Gemini button at the top right of Google Slides. Create a slide: Click Gemini → \"Create a slide about..\" It adds a slide with text and an image. Create an image: Click Gemini → \"Create an image of..\" for the current slide. Summarise: Ask Gemini to summarise the deck or suggest a better title. Rewrite text: Select text and ask Gemini to make it shorter or clearer. If you do not see the Gemini button, use Way 1. It works for everyone."},{"p":"17-gemini-slides.html","c":17,"t":"Slides with Gemini AI","h":"Gamma vs Gemini","a":"gamma-vs-gemini","x":"Gamma Gemini. Makes full deck in one click Yes No (plans it, or slide by slide). Control over content Medium High. Design Automatic, modern You choose in Google Slides. Export to PPTX Yes Yes, from Google Slides. Free Limited credits Yes. Tip: Use both. Plan with Gemini. Build with Gamma. Polish in Google Slides."},{"p":"17-gemini-slides.html","c":17,"t":"Slides with Gemini AI","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Ask Gemini for a 10-slide plan on a topic from your branch. Then build it in Google Slides. Ask Gemini to write a script for what you will say on each slide. Practise it. Put the plan into Gamma and compare the two decks. Open slides.google.com and look for the Gemini button. If it is there, make one slide with it."},{"p":"17-gemini-slides.html","c":17,"t":"Slides with Gemini AI","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Structure — the order and parts of something. Speaker notes — notes under a slide that the presenter sees but the audience does not. Deck — another word for a full set of slides. Markdown — a simple way of writing formatted text using symbols like # and -. Insert — to add something (an image, a slide) into a document. Polish — to make the final small improvements. Script — the words you will say while presenting. Workspace — Google's set of office apps: Docs, Slides, Sheets, Gmail, Drive."},{"p":"18-multimedia.html","c":18,"t":"Adding audio, video, animation and music","h":"","a":"","x":"A presentation with only text is boring. Multimedia means using images, audio, video, animation and music together. This chapter shows how to add each one with AI tools. This covers Lab experiments 15 and 16."},{"p":"18-multimedia.html","c":18,"t":"Adding audio, video, animation and music","h":"1. Images","a":"1-images","x":"You already know this from Chapter 11. Ask Gemini, ChatGPT or Canva Magic Media for an image. Say \"no text\" and the style. Insert it into the slide. For diagrams, ask: \"Draw a simple labelled diagram of a four-stroke engine, flat style, white background.\"."},{"p":"18-multimedia.html","c":18,"t":"Adding audio, video, animation and music","h":"2. Audio (narration)","a":"2-audio-narration","x":"Narration is a voice that explains the slide. You can record your own voice, or let AI speak. Record yourself: In Canva, click Record yourself under Uploads. In PowerPoint, use Insert → Audio → Record. Your phone's voice recorder also works. AI voice (text-to-speech): Write the script with Gemini: \"Write a 40-second narration for this slide in simple English.\". Turn text into speech with ElevenLabs (free plan), Google Cloud TTS demo or NaturalReader. NotebookLM's Audio Overview can also make a two-person discussion from your notes. Download the MP3. Insert it into the slide."},{"p":"18-multimedia.html","c":18,"t":"Adding audio, video, animation and music","h":"3. Video","a":"3-video","x":"Insert an existing video: In Google Slides, Insert → Video lets you paste a YouTube link. In PowerPoint, Insert → Video. In Canva, drag a video from Elements or Uploads. Make a short AI video: Gemini can make short video clips from a text prompt (with Veo), on supported plans. Google Vids makes a full video from a prompt or a document. Canva Magic Media can make short clips. Pika, Runway, Luma — free plans with limits. Keep videos short: 10 to 30 seconds per slide."},{"p":"18-multimedia.html","c":18,"t":"Adding audio, video, animation and music","h":"4. Animation and transitions","a":"4-animation-and-transitions","x":"Animation moves things on one slide. A transition is the effect between two slides. Google Slides: Select an object → Insert → Animation. Choose \"Fade in\", \"Fly in\", \"Appear\". Set \"On click\" or \"After previous\". For transitions, Slide → Transition. PowerPoint: Animations tab and Transitions tab. Canva: Click an object → Animate. For the whole page, click the page → Animate. Gamma: Animations are automatic; you can turn them on or off in the theme. Rule: use one transition for the whole deck. Too many effects look childish."},{"p":"18-multimedia.html","c":18,"t":"Adding audio, video, animation and music","h":"5. Background music","a":"5-background-music","x":"Free music: Pixabay Music, YouTube Audio Library (under Audio Library), Free Music Archive. AI-made music: Suno, Udio, MusicFX by Google. Prompt: \"Calm instrumental background music, 1 minute, no vocals.\". In Canva: Elements → Audio. Drag onto the timeline. It plays across all pages. In PowerPoint: insert the audio on slide 1, then tick Play across slides and Loop. Keep the volume low. The narration must be louder than the music."},{"p":"18-multimedia.html","c":18,"t":"Adding audio, video, animation and music","h":"Putting it together (the easy way)","a":"putting-it-together-the-easy-way","x":"Canva is the easiest tool for all of this in one place. Make the slides in Gamma or Gemini + Google Slides. Export as PPTX. Open Canva → Create a design → Presentation → Upload the PPTX (or import from PowerPoint). Add animation, narration, a video and music as above. Share → Download → MP4 Video to get a video file. Or Present from Canva."},{"p":"18-multimedia.html","c":18,"t":"Adding audio, video, animation and music","h":"Check the licence","a":"check-the-licence","x":"Always use images, music and videos that are free to use. Pixabay, Pexels, Unsplash and the YouTube Audio Library are safe. Never take music from a film or a song from YouTube; that is copyright violation. AI-generated content is fine for college work, but check each tool's rules before using it commercially."},{"p":"18-multimedia.html","c":18,"t":"Adding audio, video, animation and music","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Take the deck you made in Chapter 16 or 17. Add one AI image, one narration (your voice or AI voice), and one short video. Add animations to the text on 3 slides and one transition for the whole deck. Add a background music track from Pixabay. Export the whole thing as an MP4 from Canva. Play the MP4 on your phone. Is the narration clear? Is the music too loud?"},{"p":"18-multimedia.html","c":18,"t":"Adding audio, video, animation and music","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Multimedia — many types of media together: text, image, audio, video. Narration — a voice that speaks over the slides. Text-to-speech (TTS) — turning written text into a spoken voice. MP3 — a common audio file format. Transition — the effect when moving from one slide to the next. Timeline — the bar at the bottom of a video editor showing time. Loop — to play again and again. Instrumental — music with no singing. Licence — permission to use something; \"free to use\" means the licence allows it. Commercial — for business, to make money."},{"p":"19-ai-apps.html","c":19,"t":"Building an AI-enabled application","h":"","a":"","x":"An AI-enabled application is an app that uses an AI model inside it to do something useful. You do not need to train a model. You use a ready model (like Gemini) and build a small app around it. This is Lab experiment 17."},{"p":"19-ai-apps.html","c":19,"t":"Building an AI-enabled application","h":"Start with a real problem","a":"start-with-a-real-problem","x":"Pick a problem from real life. Keep it small. Some ideas: Field Problem What the app does. Education Students in a village cannot afford tutors Explains any 10th class topic in Telugu, with a quiz. Healthcare People do not understand their medical reports Reads a report (photo) and explains it in simple words, and says \"see a doctor\". Agriculture A farmer in Anakapalle sees spots on a plant Takes a photo and suggests what the disease may be and what to do. Marketing A small shop in Jagadamba junction needs posts Writes Instagram captions and WhatsApp messages for offers. College New students get lost on campus Answers questions about rooms, timings and bus routes."},{"p":"19-ai-apps.html","c":19,"t":"Building an AI-enabled application","h":"Three ways to build, from easy to harder","a":"three-ways-to-build-from-easy-to-harder","x":"Way 1: A custom chatbot (no code). Make a chatbot with a fixed role and instructions. Gemini Gems: In Gemini, click Gems → New Gem. Give it a name, instructions, and optionally files. Save. Now it is your app. ChatGPT custom GPTs: Similar, under Explore GPTs → Create (may need a paid plan). NotebookLM: Upload your college handbook. Share the notebook. It is now a \"campus helper\" app. Example instructions for a Gem: prompt You are \"Vizag Campus Buddy\", a helper for first year students of our college. Answer questions about college life, study tips and Visakhapatnam in simple English. If someone asks about exam dates or fees, tell them to check the college website or ask the office. Be friendly. Keep answers under 100 words.. Way 2: A small web app with Google AI Studio (little or no code). Open aistudio.google.com. Go to the Build section. Describe your app in one paragraph. Example: \"A web app where a farmer uploads a photo of a plant leaf. The app says what the problem might be and gives 3 simple steps, in English and Telugu.\". AI Studio writes the code and shows a working app. Try it. Ask for changes. You can share the link or download the code. Way 3: Write the code yourself with an API (some code). An API lets your own program talk to the AI model. You need an API key from AI Studio (free). # pip install google-genai from google import genai client = genai.Client(api_key=\"PASTE_YOUR_KEY_HERE\") topic = input(\"Which topic do you want explained? \") response = client.models.generate_content( model=\"gemini-2.5-flash\", contents=f\"Explain {topic} to a 10th class student in Telugu. Under 100 words.\", ) print(response.text). Run this in Google Colab or on your laptop. That is a complete AI app in 8 lines. Add a loop, a menu or a web page with Streamlit to make it bigger. Never share your API key. Do not paste it in WhatsApp groups or put it on GitHub. Anyone with the key can use your account."},{"p":"19-ai-apps.html","c":19,"t":"Building an AI-enabled application","h":"Design before you build","a":"design-before-you-build","x":"Write one page answering: Problem: What problem? For whom? Input: What does the user give? Text, photo, voice? AI step: What does the model do? Output: What does the user get? Risks: What if the AI is wrong? Privacy? Bias? (Chapter 6). Limits: What will the app not do? (A health app must say \"see a doctor\".)."},{"p":"19-ai-apps.html","c":19,"t":"Building an AI-enabled application","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Make a Gem or a NotebookLM for one problem from the table. Test it with 5 questions. Note 2 things it got wrong. Open AI Studio → Build and describe a small app. See what it generates. Get an API key and run the 8-line Python program in Colab. Explore Hugging Face Spaces to see thousands of small AI apps made by people. Many have their code open."},{"p":"19-ai-apps.html","c":19,"t":"Building an AI-enabled application","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Application (app) — a program made for users to do a task. AI-enabled — has an AI model inside it. Chatbot — an app you talk to by typing. Gem / custom GPT — a personal version of Gemini / ChatGPT with your own instructions. API — Application Programming Interface; a way for one program to use another program's service. API key — a secret code that identifies you when using an API. Colab — Google's free online Python notebook. Streamlit — a Python tool for making simple web apps. Risk — something that could go wrong."},{"p":"20-workflow-automation.html","c":20,"t":"AI workflow automation","h":"","a":"","x":"A workflow is a series of steps to finish a task. Automation means the steps happen by themselves, without a person clicking each one. AI workflow automation means one or more of those steps are done by AI, and the output of one step becomes the input of the next. This covers Lab experiments 18 and 19."},{"p":"20-workflow-automation.html","c":20,"t":"AI workflow automation","h":"A simple example","a":"a-simple-example","x":"Task: Every week, make a short newsletter for the class. Manual workflow: Collect the week's notices. (You). Write a summary. (You). Make a nice image. (You). Post it on the WhatsApp group. (You). Automated workflow: Notices arrive in a Google Form. (Automatic). AI summarises them into a newsletter. (AI). AI makes an image. (AI). The newsletter is emailed to the class. (Automatic). You set it up once. Then it runs every week."},{"p":"20-workflow-automation.html","c":20,"t":"AI workflow automation","h":"Chain of tools (the lab way)","a":"chain-of-tools-the-lab-way","x":"The simplest automation is a chain: the output of tool 1 goes into tool 2, and so on. In the lab you can do this by hand, to understand it. Experiment 19: Document → Summary → Presentation. Create a document. Ask Gemini to write a 2-page report on \"Electric buses for Visakhapatnam\". Paste it into Google Docs. Summarise. Give the document to NotebookLM or ChatGPT. Ask for a 10-point summary. Present. Paste the summary into Gamma using \"Paste in text\". Get a 10-slide deck. Extra steps. Ask ElevenLabs for narration. Ask Canva for animation. Ask AI for a social media post about the deck. Each tool's output is the next tool's input. That is a workflow. Write down each step and the time it took."},{"p":"20-workflow-automation.html","c":20,"t":"AI workflow automation","h":"Automation tools (no code)","a":"automation-tools-no-code","x":"These tools connect apps together. \"When X happens, do Y, then Z.\". Tool Link Notes. Zapier zapier.com Easiest. Free plan with limits. Has AI steps built in. Make make.com Visual, drag-and-drop. Free plan. n8n n8n.io Open source. Can run on your own laptop. Strong AI features. Google Apps Script script.google.com Free. Automates Gmail, Sheets, Docs with small code. Gemini can write the code. IFTTT ifttt.com Simple \"if this, then that\" rules. Experiment 18 example with Zapier or Make: Trigger: A new row is added to a Google Sheet (a student's question). AI step: Send the question to ChatGPT or Gemini with a prompt: \"Answer this in simple English in under 50 words.\". Action: Write the answer in the next column of the sheet. Or send it by email. Many such tools have a free plan with 100 runs a month. That is enough for the lab."},{"p":"20-workflow-automation.html","c":20,"t":"AI workflow automation","h":"Automation with a little code","a":"automation-with-a-little-code","x":"With an API key (Chapter 19) and Python, you can write your own automation. Example: a Python script that reads all.txt files in a folder, asks Gemini to summarise each one, and saves a summary.txt. Ask an AI to write this script for you. Google Apps Script is also powerful. Ask Gemini: \"Write a Google Apps Script that, for every row in my sheet, sends column A to Gemini and writes the answer in column B.\"."},{"p":"20-workflow-automation.html","c":20,"t":"AI workflow automation","h":"AI agents","a":"ai-agents","x":"The newest kind of automation is the AI agent. An agent is an AI that can plan steps and use tools on its own. You say the goal. It decides the steps. Examples: Gemini's and ChatGPT's agent modes, Claude Code, OpenAI Operator, Manus. Agents are new and still make mistakes, so a human must check the result."},{"p":"20-workflow-automation.html","c":20,"t":"AI workflow automation","h":"Things to remember","a":"things-to-remember","x":"Start by doing the workflow by hand once. Then automate it. Keep a human check at the end for anything important. Automation that sends emails or messages can make mistakes at scale. Test with yourself first. Do not automate anything that needs personal data without permission."},{"p":"20-workflow-automation.html","c":20,"t":"AI workflow automation","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"Do the Document → Summary → Presentation chain above with 3 different tools. Write down each step. Sign up at zapier.com or make.com. Make a 3-step zap: Google Form → AI summary → Gmail. Test it with yourself as the receiver. Open script.google.com and ask Gemini to write a script that emails you a joke every morning. Watch: n8n in 100 seconds — search for a short beginner video."},{"p":"20-workflow-automation.html","c":20,"t":"AI workflow automation","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Workflow — a series of steps to complete a task. Automation — making steps happen without a person doing each one. Chain — a line of tools where each one's output feeds the next. Trigger — the event that starts an automation (\"a new form response\"). Action — a step the automation does (\"send an email\"). Zap / scenario — one automation in Zapier / Make. Open source — software whose code is free to see, use and change. Agent — an AI that plans its own steps and uses tools to reach a goal. At scale — in large numbers; a mistake repeated many times."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"","a":"","x":"Generative AI changes every few months. This chapter lists the main directions. Some are already here. Some are coming. This is Lab experiment 20."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"1. AI agents","a":"1-ai-agents","x":"Today you ask the AI one question and get one answer. Tomorrow you will give a goal, and the AI will do many steps on its own: search, read, write code, run it, fix it, send an email. These are called agents. Early agents already exist for coding, shopping and research. Engineers will spend more time checking AI work and less time doing it by hand."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"2. Multimodal everything","a":"2-multimodal-everything","x":"Models now see, hear and speak. Soon they will live in glasses, cars, earphones and robots. You will point your phone camera at a machine and ask \"what is this part?\" You will talk to AI in Telugu and it will reply in Telugu, with the right accent."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"3. Small models on your device","a":"3-small-models-on-your-device","x":"Big models run in data centres. Small language models (SLMs) run on a phone or a laptop with no internet. Examples: Gemini Nano on Android phones, Apple's on-device models, Llama and Gemma that you can run with Ollama. This is faster, cheaper and private. Good for villages with poor internet."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"4. Indian language AI","a":"4-indian-language-ai","x":"India has 22 official languages. Most AI was trained mostly on English. This is changing fast. Bhashini (bhashini.gov.in) — the government's AI translation platform. Sarvam AI, AI4Bharat (IIT Madras), Krutrim — Indian companies and labs building Telugu, Hindi and other language models. The IndiaAI Mission is funding GPUs and Indian models. Engineers from Andhra Pradesh who know Telugu and English well will be needed for this work."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"5. AI in science and engineering","a":"5-ai-in-science-and-engineering","x":"AlphaFold (Google DeepMind) predicted the shape of almost every known protein. It won a Nobel Prize in 2024. AI is designing new materials, batteries and medicines. AI helps weather forecasting. This matters for cyclones on the Vizag coast. In civil, mechanical and electrical engineering, AI is used for design, simulation and predicting when machines will fail."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"6. Video and 3D generation","a":"6-video-and-3d-generation","x":"Text-to-video models (Veo, Sora, Kling) are getting good enough for advertisements and short films. 3D model generation is coming. This will change film, gaming and architecture."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"7. AI and jobs","a":"7-ai-and-jobs","x":"Some jobs will change a lot: writing, customer support, basic coding, design. New jobs will appear: prompt engineers, AI trainers, AI safety testers, AI product builders. The safest plan is to be the person who uses AI well in your own field."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"8. Rules and safety","a":"8-rules-and-safety","x":"Governments are making laws. The EU AI Act is the first big one. India has the DPDP Act for data. Companies are adding watermarks to AI images and videos so people can tell what is fake. Expect more rules about deepfakes, copyright and AI in exams."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"9. Open models","a":"9-open-models","x":"Some companies release their models for free: Meta's Llama, Google's Gemma, Mistral, DeepSeek, Qwen. Anyone can download and run them. This lets students, startups and colleges build without paying per use. See huggingface.co."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"10. The cost is falling","a":"10-the-cost-is-falling","x":"In 2023, using a top model cost a lot. Now a model of the same quality costs 100 times less. This means AI will be in every app, even small ones made by students."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"How to keep learning","a":"how-to-keep-learning","x":"Follow one good source: Google AI blog, OpenAI news, Anthropic news. Try one new tool a month. Build small things. A Gem, a script, a workflow. Join a college AI club or a hackathon. Many are free."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"Try it","a":"try-it","x":"This is Lab experiment 20. Pick one recent AI technology. Examples: NotebookLM Audio Overview, Gemini Live, Veo video, Ollama on your laptop, Bhashini, AlphaFold, an AI agent. Use it yourself for 30 minutes. Prepare a 5-slide demo or a one-page report with: what it does, how it works (simple), 3 uses, 3 benefits, and 3 responsible-AI concerns (Chapter 6). Install Ollama and run a small model on your laptop with no internet. Ask it a question in Telugu. Explore huggingface.co/spaces and find one demo from an Indian lab."},{"p":"21-future-trends.html","c":21,"t":"Future trends in Generative AI","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Trend — a general direction in which things are changing. Agent — an AI that can plan and act on its own to reach a goal. On-device — running on your phone or laptop, not on the internet. SLM — Small Language Model; a small version that runs on normal devices. Data centre — a building full of computers where big AI models run. Open model — a model whose weights are free to download and use. Watermark — a hidden mark in an image or video that shows it was made by AI. Simulation — using a computer to imitate a real process, like a bridge under load. Hackathon — a short event where teams build a project in a day or two."},{"p":"22-lab-guide.html","c":22,"t":"Lab experiments guide","h":"","a":"","x":"The lab has 20 experiments. This page gives, for each one, the chapter to read, the tools to use, and what to put in your record. Keep it open during the lab."},{"p":"22-lab-guide.html","c":22,"t":"Lab experiments guide","h":"What to write in your lab record","a":"what-to-write-in-your-lab-record","x":"For every experiment, write these five things: Aim — one line. Tools used — with links. Prompts — the exact text you typed. Copy and paste. Output — screenshots or pasted text. If long, paste the important part. Observation — 3 to 5 lines in your own words. What worked? What was wrong? What did you learn?"},{"p":"22-lab-guide.html","c":22,"t":"Lab experiments guide","h":"The 20 experiments","a":"the-20-experiments","x":"# Experiment Read Tools Key point. 1 Explore Generative AI tools: same question in ChatGPT, Gemini, NotebookLM, AI Studio Ch 5 All four Make a table comparing the four answers. 2 AI-based information generation: explain an engineering topic and evaluate it Ch 3, 7 Gemini or ChatGPT Rate relevance, accuracy, completeness out of 5, with reasons. 3 Document analysis with NotebookLM: summary, key points, questions Ch 5, 9 NotebookLM Upload a PDF; note which page each answer came from. 4 Responsible AI analysis: bias, misinformation, privacy, copyright Ch 6 Any chat tool One prompt and one observation for each of the four issues. 5 Basic prompt engineering: zero-shot, role-based, structured Ch 7, 8 Any chat tool Same problem, three prompts, compare outputs. 6 AI-assisted writing and summarization Ch 9 Any chat tool Article (300 words) plus summary (3 bullets, 1 line). 7 Context-based document creation: notice, report, invitation Ch 10 Any chat tool, Google Docs Write the context checklist first. 8 Greeting card with image and animation Ch 11 Gemini/ChatGPT for image, Canva Export as MP4 or GIF. 9 AI-based program generation in C/Python/Java Ch 12 Any chat tool, OnlineGDB Run it; test normal, edge and wrong inputs. 10 AI-assisted debugging with a second tool Ch 13 Two different chat tools List the bugs you planted and which were found. 11 Programming language conversion Ch 14 Any chat tool, OnlineGDB Same inputs in both languages; outputs must match. 12 Test case generation: normal, boundary, invalid, edge Ch 15 Any chat tool Test table with Expected, Actual and Pass columns. 13 PPT generation with Gamma Ch 16 Gamma Export PPTX; check facts. 14 PPT with Gemini: content/structure then presentation Ch 17 Gemini, Google Slides Slide plan with speaker notes. 15 Multimedia-enhanced presentation: images, audio, video Ch 18 Canva, ElevenLabs, Pixabay Note the licence of each media file. 16 Animated presentation: animations, transitions, narration, music Ch 18 Canva or PowerPoint One transition for the whole deck; export MP4. 17 Develop an AI-enabled application Ch 19 Gemini Gems, AI Studio Build, or Python + API One-page design: problem, input, AI step, output, risks. 18 AI workflow automation: output of one step feeds the next Ch 20 Zapier / Make / Apps Script Draw the flow: trigger → AI step → action. 19 Generative AI productivity workflow: document → summary → presentation Ch 20 Gemini, NotebookLM, Gamma Write each step and the time it took. 20 Future trends: explore a recent technology, demo or report Ch 21 Your choice Include 3 responsible-AI concerns."},{"p":"22-lab-guide.html","c":22,"t":"Lab experiments guide","h":"Links in one place","a":"links-in-one-place","x":"Chat tools. ChatGPT — chatgpt.com. Gemini — gemini.google.com. NotebookLM — notebooklm.google.com. Google AI Studio — aistudio.google.com. Claude — claude.ai. Copilot — copilot.microsoft.com. Design and slides. Canva — canva.com. Gamma — gamma.app. Google Slides — slides.google.com. Microsoft Designer — designer.microsoft.com. Code. OnlineGDB — onlinegdb.com. Programiz — programiz.com. Google Colab — colab.research.google.com. CodeXRay — codexray-frontend.onrender.com. Audio, video, music. ElevenLabs — elevenlabs.io. Pixabay — pixabay.com. Google Vids — vids.google.com. Automation. Zapier — zapier.com. Make — make.com. n8n — n8n.io. Google Apps Script — script.google.com. Learning more. Google prompting guide — ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/prompting-strategies. Hugging Face — huggingface.co. Ollama — ollama.com. Bhashini — bhashini.gov.in."},{"p":"22-lab-guide.html","c":22,"t":"Lab experiments guide","h":"New words","a":"new-words","x":"Aim — the goal of an experiment. Observation — what you noticed; written in your own words. Record — the notebook where you write all your lab work. Screenshot — a picture of your screen. Evaluate — to judge how good something is, with reasons. Relevance — how well the answer matches the question. Completeness — whether anything important is missing."}];
